  <h1>Dog Bite Law Guides</h1>
  <p>Plain-language guides written and reviewed by editorial staff with experience in personal injury litigation and dog bite claims. Each guide provides the legal and practical context behind the calculator results.</p>

  <h2><a href="/guides/how-dog-bite-claims-work/">How Dog Bite Claims Work</a></h2>
  <p>The complete process from bite to settlement: establishing liability under strict liability or the one-bite rule, filing the homeowners insurance claim, building the damages case, and what drives settlement value beyond medical bills. Covers the negotiation process, when to file suit, and how attorney representation affects outcomes.</p>

  <h2><a href="/guides/dog-bite-liability-by-state/">Dog Bite Liability by State</a></h2>
  <p>Which states use strict liability statutes and which follow the one-bite rule or negligence standard — and how the distinction affects your recovery and litigation strategy. Covers state-specific exceptions (trespasser exclusions, professional handler exceptions, breed-specific provisions) and the local leash ordinance theory available in most states regardless of the general liability framework.</p>

  <h2><a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-dog-bite/">What to Do After a Dog Bite</a></h2>
  <p>Step-by-step guidance for the critical first hours and days: immediate medical care, identifying the dog and documenting vaccination status, rabies protocol, reporting to animal control, evidence preservation, and when to consult a personal injury attorney. The steps taken in the 48 hours after a bite often determine whether a claim is viable and how strong it is.</p>

  <h2><a href="/guides/dog-bite-misconceptions/">Common Dog Bite Claim Misconceptions</a></h2>
  <p>Five widely held misunderstandings about dog bite law: the "one free bite" rule and when prior history matters, breed exclusions in homeowners insurance policies, the provocation defense and what actually counts as provocation, statute of limitations myths, and the full scope of recoverable damages beyond medical bills.</p>

